const T2_OPENER = `<p><b>Interviewee</b> Believe it or not, this is a clock. It doesn’t have any clock hands or numbers, but it has three concentric rings which light up to show the hour, minute and second so that it tells you the time. The grasshopper on top moves, appearing to “eat time” as the seconds pass by.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Where is the clock?</p><p><b>Interviewee</b> It’s known as the “Grasshopper clock”, and it’s outside the Taylor Library at Corpus Christi College in Cambridge. It was unveiled in 2008 by Stephen Hawking, an expert on the physics of time.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Why is time important?</p><p><b>Interviewee</b> The length of a second never changes, and yet we feel time passing differently depending on whether we’re asleep or awake, and whether we are bored or having fun. Physics also tells us that time passes slower when you are moving at a higher speed, especially when you travel close to the speed of light.</p>`;
const T2_LIAM = `<p><b>Presenter</b> In today’s Youth Matters, we wanted to ask: are today’s generation of young people going to be better leaders? In the studio today we have Liam and his mum, Kathy.</p><p><b>Liam</b> Hey. <b>Mum</b> Hiya.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> So, you’re 16, right, Liam? <b>Liam</b> Yes, and 17 in October.</p><p><b>Presenter</b> Oh, so <b>this</b> year you can start to drive, and <b>next</b> year you can vote. Kathy, you’re Generation X, correct? Are you worried about Liam’s generation…?</p><p><b>Mum</b> Yes. I’m not sure wasting time using social media or playing computer games is going to make the world better. I also worry that spending all that time online isn’t good. I think over time it makes a lot of young people feel anxious because they always compare their lives to the lives of their friends or the rich and famous! I don’t know… I’m worried that they’re all too anxious to become leaders.</p><p><b>Liam</b> First of all, using social media is not <b>wasting</b> time; it’s just <b>spending</b> time doing something I enjoy. And I think when we’re older, the world will be a better place, not worse. We already lead change, and social media is an important way to reach people. I read that it’s good to spend time on social media because you’re thinking about other people; it’s important to know that it’s not all about you. On social media, you learn to be part of a community. We’re good at making time for other people and thinking about things from their perspective. But from what Mum tells me, they were too worried about being cool!</p><p><b>Mum</b> That’s probably true! When I was your age, I think it was important for us to find our own style and identity. We knew what we liked, and we expressed that through clothes and music. Generation Z’s fashion trends change extremely quickly and it’s a shame because that made us strong and confident, then we felt more independent. We used to love going to parties, travelling, and experiencing things in the real world… Liam’s generation is always connected, always online, and I worry they can’t make decisions alone.</p><p><b>Liam</b> Personally, I don’t think it’s good to make decisions alone. I feel that a good leader represents the views of many people from different backgrounds, so to be a good leader, you need to listen – that helps you to make better decisions.</p><p><b>Mum</b> That’s a really good point – I totally agree. My friends and I knew about politics and we did do some things… But… generally, I don’t think we had much time for politics; issues like climate change didn’t really interest us. I guess we were quite lazy. Generation Z is actually quite active and interested in world issues.</p><p><b>Liam</b> Exactly! Generation Z is making the world better.</p>`;
const T2_135 = `<p><b>Are you a lark or an owl?</b> The time of day affects how we feel because of circadian rhythms, also known as “the body clock”. These are body changes that happen every twenty-four hours.</p><p>Circadian rhythms include changes in body temperature, heart rate, and strength, which affect many physical activities. For example, there’s a decrease in our body temperature overnight, but it recovers gradually throughout the day. When scientists studied athletes, they found that runners, cyclists, and swimmers did best between 4.30 p.m. and 5.30 p.m., especially for activities that lasted a short time. Those results were probably a benefit of warmer muscles as body temperature rises during the day. However, fencers performed better in the middle of the day, perhaps because their sport demands mental skills, which decline after noon.</p><p>Researchers used to think people had similar circadian rhythms, but recent studies showed that personality and environment affect someone’s body clock. For example, shy people often function better in the morning, while outgoing people are better later in the day. And in 2015, a study showed that people who liked to wake up early and work in the morning performed better early in the day than those who preferred to wake up later and work in the evenings.</p>`;
const T2_136 = `<p><b>Miriam</b> Hi, I’m Miriam Alter. Welcome to Mind What You Say, a podcast about your mind, your body, and your feelings. Today, our guest is Kevin Jameson, an author who writes about mindfulness. There’s been a big growth in mindfulness research lately and Kevin’s here to tell us about how mindfulness affects our sense of time. Kevin, welcome.</p>`;
const T2_137 = T2_136 + `<p><b>Kevin</b> Thank you, Miriam. Happy to be here.</p><p><b>Miriam</b> First, what exactly is mindfulness?</p><p><b>Kevin</b> Great question. Basically, mindfulness involves slowing down and focusing on the present.</p><p><b>Miriam</b> That’s so hard. How do we do that?</p><p><b>Kevin</b> First, it’s important to pay attention to the present moment and what is happening in your body. Think about breathing in and out. Be aware of your thoughts and feelings, but don’t judge them.</p><p><b>Miriam</b> And how can mindfulness help us?</p><p><b>Kevin</b> Well, in lots of ways – we feel less anxious, and it helps us to focus over time. But I’m really interested in how it changes the way we understand or perceive time. A recent UK study reported a big effect on this. Researchers showed participants shapes for a very short time. Then for ten minutes, half the participants listened to a story, and half to a mindfulness exercise. Then the researchers showed the participants the shapes again. Those who listened to the mindfulness exercise said the shapes appeared for a longer time than those who listened to a story. When they were being mindful, their perception of time slowed down.</p><p><b>Miriam</b> Mindfulness actually slowed their perception of time?</p><p><b>Kevin</b> Exactly! Isn’t that amazing? I used to think I needed more time. Now that I make time to practise mindfulness, it feels like I have time to do more.</p>`;

const EDDIE = [
  ['Eddie', 'Hey Dani! Are you ready for this talk in the library?'], ['Dani', 'Oh yeah! I just need two minutes.'],
  ['Eddie', 'Speaking of the library, guess what happened to me there the other day.'], ['Dani', 'What?'],
  ['Eddie', 'This is so embarrassing! You know the library’s got those big glass doors? They were open because they were cleaning them.'], ['Dani', 'OK…'],
  ['Eddie', 'So, anyway, I was studying at one of the tables when my phone rang and it was my Dad. I went outside to answer it and closed one of the doors behind me. After I hung up, I went back in. But I forgot the door was closed.'],
  ['Dani', 'Oh no, I think I know what you’re going to say!'],
  ['Eddie', 'Yep. So, what happened was the glass was so clean, I didn’t see the door, and I walked straight into it. It didn’t break but there was this loud crash, and everybody looked up!'],
  ['Dani', 'Oh, Eddie!'],
  ['Eddie', 'I know, I know. At first, they were all shocked, and maybe a bit worried I was hurt, but after a while they all started laughing. And I mean the whole library! The funny thing was that I closed the door! I just completely forgot!'],
  ['Dani', 'That reminds me of the time I thought a stranger was my sister and I tried to give her a hug. Or did I ever tell you about the time I thought a party was fancy dress but it wasn’t?!'],
  ['Eddie', 'Er, no! You need to tell me both of these stories!'], ['Dani', 'OK, but after the talk!']
];
const eddieHtml = () => '<div class="dlg">' + EDDIE.map(([w, t]) => `<p><b>${w}</b> ${Core.esc(t)}</p>`).join('') + '</div>';

const FALSE_MEM = [
  'It’s unusual for us to remember the **past** completely accurately, but false memories are very common. False memories can be particularly rich in detail – details that our brain **makes up**.',
  'Rob Nash is a psychologist at Aston University. He studies false memories and is an expert in them. Nash remembers that he was extremely excited to meet the British television celebrity Trevor McDonald at his sister’s graduation. Nash was sitting right at the back of the hall when the TV presenter appeared on the stage. McDonald was wearing colourful graduation clothes and Nash listened while McDonald was giving a speech.',
  'Afterwards, Nash, had the chance to meet McDonald in person. But a few years later, Nash **discovered** that he was never at his sister’s graduation – and neither was Trevor McDonald.',
  'It’s strange that Nash didn’t **recognise** that these were false memories. But he is not alone. In an interview with the British newspaper *The Times*, the writer Ian McEwan described a similar false memory of a beautiful book. He remembered that he wrote the book and when he moved house, he put the book in a drawer. One day, he decided to look for it and he **searched** everywhere for it! In his **mind**, he could see the papers, the folder they were in, the drawer. His mind **convinced** him that it existed.',
  'He couldn’t escape the truth – those events never happened. The book didn’t exist.',
  'So, why do we have false memories?',
  'But over the past decade, psychologists have started to **realise** that false memories can actually be useful for us. For example, they can prepare us for possible events beyond our experience. Also, memories (**accurate** or false) of special people from our past can help us to feel close to them.'
];
const falseMemHtml = () => '<div class="article"><div class="art-kicker">False Memories</div><p>' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[0]) + ' ¹____ ' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[1]) + ' ²____ ' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[2]) + ' ³____</p><p>' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[3]) + ' ⁴____ ' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[4]) + '</p><p>' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[5]) + ' ⁵____ ' + Core.md(FALSE_MEM[6]) + '</p><p class="src">Adapted from ‘False memories: why we experience tricks of the mind’ by Philip Ball, Science Focus, September 2019</p></div>';
const SEL_AF = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F'];
const ROSETTA = [
  'A long time ago, in ancient Egypt people used to write with hieroglyphs. These were pictures which represented words. They used to paint them on the paper they made from dry papyrus (a plant). Sometimes they cut hieroglyphs into stones.',
  'In July 1799, French soldiers discovered one of these stones in Rosetta, Egypt, after they took control of the region. Before the discovery, we didn’t use to understand Egyptian hieroglyphs. The stone was from 196 BCE and it contained a message in hieroglyphs and two other languages. Afterwards, language experts started comparing them. Later, in 1822, Jean-François Champollion realised that the Egyptian text symbols represented sounds, not just meanings. For example, you could write belief with the symbols for bee and leaf. This helped people to translate it.',
  'The Rosetta Stone helped the world to understand ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs and learn about ancient Egyptian culture.'
];
const rosettaHtml = () => '<div class="article green"><div class="art-kicker">REFERENCE POINT · HISTORY › ARTIFACTS</div><h4 class="art-h">The Rosetta Stone</h4>' + ROSETTA.map(p => '<p>' + Core.esc(p) + '</p>').join('') + '<p class="src">Фото: пример иероглифов и папирус — в учебнике с. 24.</p></div>';

